problem:
Problem description
- A single-period transportation planning problem is considered over a monthly planning horizon $$\mathcal{T}$$.
- There is a set of supply nodes $$\mathcal{I}$$ representing coal yards, and a set of demand nodes $$\mathcal{J}$$ representing residential areas.
- Each supply node $$i \in \mathcal{I}$$ has an available coal quantity $$s_i$$ for the planning horizon.
- Each demand node $$j \in \mathcal{J}$$ has a required coal quantity $$d_j$$ for the planning horizon.
- The transportation distance from supply node $$i$$ to demand node $$j$$ is denoted by $$\ell_{ij}$$.
- The planning goal is to determine how much coal to ship from each supply node to each demand node so that demand is satisfied, supply availability is respected, and total ton-kilometers are minimized.
- The business rules are:
  - Coal shipped out of any supply node must not exceed the amount available at that node.
  - Each demand node must receive enough coal to meet its monthly requirement.
  - Shipment quantities must be nonnegative.
  - When the network is balanced, all available supply is allocated and all demand is met.
- A solution is represented by:
  - $$x_{ij}$$: the quantity of coal shipped from supply node $$i \in \mathcal{I}$$ to demand node $$j \in \mathcal{J}$$ over the planning horizon.
  - $$Z$$: the total transportation burden measured in ton-kilometers.

Input Format
{
  "planning_horizon": {
    "type": "set",
    "symbol": "$$\\mathcal{T}$$",
    "meaning": "Monthly planning horizon."
  },
  "supply_nodes": {
    "type": "set",
    "symbol": "$$\\mathcal{I}$$",
    "meaning": "Set of coal yards."
  },
  "demand_nodes": {
    "type": "set",
    "symbol": "$$\\mathcal{J}$$",
    "meaning": "Set of residential areas."
  },
  "supply_availability": {
    "type": "vector",
    "symbol": "$$s_i$$",
    "shape": "$$|\\mathcal{I}|$$",
    "meaning": "Available coal quantity at each supply node $$i \\in \\mathcal{I}$$."
  },
  "demand_requirements": {
    "type": "vector",
    "symbol": "$$d_j$$",
    "shape": "$$|\\mathcal{J}|$$",
    "meaning": "Required coal quantity at each demand node $$j \\in \\mathcal{J}$$."
  },
  "distance_matrix": {
    "type": "matrix",
    "symbol": "$$\\ell_{ij}$$",
    "shape": "$$|\\mathcal{I}| \\times |\\mathcal{J}|$$",
    "meaning": "Distance in kilometers from supply node $$i$$ to demand node $$j$$."
  }
}

Output Format
{
  "shipments": {
    "type": "matrix",
    "symbol": "$$x_{ij}$$",
    "shape": "$$|\\mathcal{I}| \\times |\\mathcal{J}|$$",
    "meaning": "Coal shipment quantity from supply node $$i$$ to demand node $$j$$."
  },
  "total_transportation_burden": {
    "type": "scalar",
    "symbol": "$$Z$$",
    "meaning": "Total ton-kilometers produced by the shipment plan."
  }
}

Example(s)

Input 1
{
  "planning_horizon": 1,
  "supply_nodes": ["A", "B"],
  "demand_nodes": ["R1", "R2", "R3"],
  "supply_availability": {
    "A": 80,
    "B": 100
  },
  "demand_requirements": {
    "R1": 55,
    "R2": 75,
    "R3": 50
  },
  "distance_matrix": {
    "A": {
      "R1": 10,
      "R2": 5,
      "R3": 6
    },
    "B": {
      "R1": 4,
      "R2": 8,
      "R3": 15
    }
  }
}
